          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe about starting a statin, and which risk bands are used?",
          "answer": "Guidance regarding starting statin therapy varies based on risk factors and established conditions. According to the document titled **\"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\"**, the following groups are indicated for statin therapy:\n\n1. **Established atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease** - high-intensity statin therapy for secondary prevention.\n2. [removed: this system does not give doses or amounts.]\n3. **Diabetes, aged 40 to 75** - at least moderate-intensity statin therapy.\n4. **Intermediate risk** - moderate-intensity statin therapy after a risk discussion.\n5. **Borderline risk** - a risk discussion is recommended; therapy is favored if risk-enhancing features are present (e.g., family history of premature heart disease) (source: guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation).\n\nFor risk band classifications, the same guideline outlines the estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk as follows:\n\n- **Low risk**: below 5 percent\n- **Borderline risk**: 5 to below 7.5 percent\n- **Intermediate risk**: 7.5 to below 20 percent\n- **High risk**: 20 percent or above\n\nRisk-enhancing features may influence treatment decisions within the borderline and intermediate risk categories (source: guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation).\n\nThis framework helps clinicians determine when to initiate statin therapy based on individual risk assessments.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
